{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"symmetric encryption symmetric encryption","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md","fileName":"Symmetric Encryption.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Symmetric Encryption","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Cryptography and Access Control]] [[Applied Cryptography and PKI]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Cryptography and Access Control","Applied Cryptography and PKI","Asymmetric Encryption","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf","cybersecurity.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"symmetric encryption symmetric encryption","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Symmetric Encryption.md","fileName":"Symmetric Encryption.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Symmetric Encryption","heading":"Symmetric Encryption","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Symmetric encryption uses the same secret key to encrypt plaintext and decrypt ciphertext. It is efficient for protecting substantial amounts of data, but every party that needs access must securely obtain and protect the shared key.\n\nConfidentiality alone does not prove that encrypted data was not modified. A complete design must use an authenticated construction or pair encryption with appropriate integrity protection, while keeping keys separate from the data they protect.\n\nSymmetric algorithms are comparatively fast and efficient for large volumes of data. Their main operational challenge is distributing the shared secret without exposing it, so secure protocols can use [[